#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105475 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b13a2e9-d7e8-51a3-b452-0594858dd0d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/id #uuid "e7b51055-36f2-552b-817e-3632cf0446b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12924" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given that the first term is $-1$ and the eighth term is $13.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is $a_1=-1,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_1=-1$ and $a_8=13$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{1}}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-1)}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + (n-1) (2) \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + 2n - 2 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 2n - 3.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23171184-edaf-560e-a5a6-fd7f0be2f6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "c7bb25e3-0f81-5290-970d-6785ecbc921c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12925" :question/problem "The fifth and sixth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $46$ and $50,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms $a_5=46$ and $a_6=50$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{6}-{a}_{5} \\\\  & =50-46 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_5=46,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_5$ is $4$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_5 &= a_1 + 4d \\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 + 4\\cdot 4\\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 +16 \\[3pt]\n46-16 &= a_1\\[3pt]\n      a_1&= 30.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = 30 + (n-1) \\cdot 4 \\[3pt]\n \t\t& =30 + 4n -4 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 4n +26.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97cdef9-0f97-5f2e-a168-2ea62e08a7f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "e564d562-6dd0-5736-a539-63b6c6592e81" :question/math-academy-id "e-12927" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term $a_{3}=9$ and the $8$th term is $a_{8}=69.$ Find a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out what the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ are.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_{8}=69$ and $a_{3}=9$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{3}}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{69-9}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_{3}=9,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+2\\cdot 12 \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+24 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-15+(n-1)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-15+12n-12 \\\\  & =12n-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23171184-edaf-560e-a5a6-fd7f0be2f6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29d8449f-a2a9-5f5d-8194-1fb9fc01dd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=29-6n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526590} {:answer/id #uuid "39dd4436-0462-52a9-8a76-ff3378d47742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=36-7n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526612} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd38354-9ff1-5302-951a-56937f006954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=29-7n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526691} {:answer/id #uuid "8890407d-9b09-50bd-93bb-97a5aad99850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=36-8n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526775} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d5b5fe-9067-555e-9c87-d2f8bc76fbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=43-8n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39dd4436-0462-52a9-8a76-ff3378d47742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=36-7n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526612} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ee1412-300a-5b7c-8672-5b2fe0bb6427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1617bc78-d7dd-5411-87bd-2e3b8377e377" :question/math-academy-id "q-177698" :question/problem "The $7$th and $8$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $-13$ and $-20,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{7}=-13$ and ${a}_{8}=-20$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-20-(-13) \\\\  & =-20+13 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{8}=-20,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{8}$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}+7(-7) \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}-49 \\\\ -20+49 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =29+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =29-7n+7 \\\\  & =36-7n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97cdef9-0f97-5f2e-a168-2ea62e08a7f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38603c9f-ce2f-5228-bc82-cd69f03c4051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526609} {:answer/id #uuid "942de8a9-dc38-58f8-b919-a9c435176e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526792} {:answer/id #uuid "a74d176b-cd65-53b6-99a3-de18df35d996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526826} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cce27d-ad6b-5b49-88ad-8ff0f21d9e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526840} {:answer/id #uuid "cd62b352-c3ca-5fbf-8c06-9fd7dbe35fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38603c9f-ce2f-5228-bc82-cd69f03c4051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526609} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f892e58-152a-5784-8094-f9feb6a190be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "280035db-18a9-57e4-8578-03884e2e2b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-177715" :question/problem "Find the $14$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $3$rd term is $40$ and the $23$rd term is $-20.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=40$ and ${a}_{23}=-20$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{23}-{a}_{3}}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-40}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-60)}{20} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=40,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}+2(-3) \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}-6 \\\\ 40+6 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =46+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =46-3n+3 \\\\  & =49-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $14$th term by substituting $n=14$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{14} & =49-3(14) \\\\  & =49-42 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b13a2e9-d7e8-51a3-b452-0594858dd0d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15902cea-7437-56a5-9a4e-bef728799b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=3-3n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526546} {:answer/id #uuid "4baadab2-f74e-5b1a-a566-4516ff320bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=10-4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526658} {:answer/id #uuid "74b90514-f9b6-53a8-9a0e-c8f08899b1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-4-2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526730} {:answer/id #uuid "c40605ec-6e8c-5d86-88f7-e1a707947506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-25+n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526876} {:answer/id #uuid "d61be220-e0b3-5cf5-ba3d-fb0da48220f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-32+2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4baadab2-f74e-5b1a-a566-4516ff320bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=10-4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526658} :answer-field/id #uuid "513c7915-c92e-5aaa-be0c-6e034ccddc23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bf79e97-49c2-5006-b045-7fdc65e739fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-5430" :question/problem "Find the general formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $7$th term is $-18$ and the first term is $6.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=6,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=6$ and ${a}_{7}=-18$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{1}}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18-6}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{6} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the formula for the $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =6+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =6-4n+4 \\\\  & =10-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97cdef9-0f97-5f2e-a168-2ea62e08a7f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2cd7eb-ba98-5414-a771-7bc2d60594d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526562} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6ce4a8-5eae-5199-9d8b-b540d1db3c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526593} {:answer/id #uuid "4a64c24b-78bb-5b96-a92b-a82e8b8b6cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526655} {:answer/id #uuid "53cdf364-07fb-51ec-b7c3-c207679b3f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526672} {:answer/id #uuid "db55de2e-167e-50d1-b5d8-8a688cc5cb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6ce4a8-5eae-5199-9d8b-b540d1db3c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526593} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf5cb02-1913-55ab-ada6-fd6ff2e54598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae437285-daa1-5ee1-861c-3f25e99e891f" :question/math-academy-id "q-5436" :question/problem "Find the  $11$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $19$th term is $99$ and the $4$th term is $-6.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{19}=99$ and ${a}_{4}=-6$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{19}-{a}_{4}}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{99-(-6)}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{15} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{4}=-6,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{4}$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+3\\cdot 7 \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-27+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7n-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $11$th term by substituting $n=11$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & =7(11)-34 \\\\  & =77-34 \\\\  & =43\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b13a2e9-d7e8-51a3-b452-0594858dd0d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c984f91-a4b9-5814-b699-528b5d5712e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5-5n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526659} {:answer/id #uuid "73277f8a-4dc6-5409-8fd7-120803fdac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526727} {:answer/id #uuid "89f223b1-2aae-5a28-93a0-2e2fa10cbab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526780} {:answer/id #uuid "c093cbe9-ccb0-5011-bba5-8f02aef40b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526870} {:answer/id #uuid "e0390ac1-96dc-5f85-a891-92dd602cb026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73277f8a-4dc6-5409-8fd7-120803fdac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526727} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b7787d-35b2-532c-8902-7f97ae2f70d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a8909ee-fe18-5d80-9c79-9f98ec446962" :question/math-academy-id "q-67258" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $1$st term is $0$ and the $9$th term is $40.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=0,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=0$ and ${a}_{9}=40$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{1}}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-0}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =0+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =5n-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23171184-edaf-560e-a5a6-fd7f0be2f6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d8b2ad-600e-5ebd-8a78-d74cb33e71e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=9n+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526558} {:answer/id #uuid "468640b9-af11-542d-b37d-d31de6796ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=20n+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526638} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8ed075-b778-5d73-9752-f895a8ed1f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526807} {:answer/id #uuid "aef36b79-0fca-55a0-82c2-a387aff7f592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=35n-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526835} {:answer/id #uuid "be92e317-fad1-556b-9b81-60c3646e4319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=15n+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef36b79-0fca-55a0-82c2-a387aff7f592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=35n-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526835} :answer-field/id #uuid "43336448-fdb3-5bd6-87e6-58e2f3e34475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e88dff2d-42e3-57bd-b14e-035af9fb2260" :question/math-academy-id "q-67315" :question/problem "The second and third terms of an arithmetic sequence are $45$ and $80,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{2}=45$ and ${a}_{3}=80$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{3}-{a}_{2} \\\\  & =80-45 \\\\  & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{2}=45,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{2}$ is $1$ jump after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1\\cdot d \\\\ 45 & ={a}_{1}+35 \\\\ 45-35 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =10+35n-35 \\\\  & =35n-25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
